Troubleshooting Tip: Ping vs Telnet

When debugging connectivity issues, two simple commands often save the day:

🔹 Ping → Uses ICMP to check if the server is reachable and measure latency.
🔹 Telnet → Uses TCP to test if a specific port/service (like SSH on 22 or SQL on 1433) is open and listening.

>Think of it this way:
	•	Ping = Is the server alive?
	•	Telnet = Is the service alive on that server?

>Example:
If ping to a database server works but telnet <IP> 1433 fails → the server is up, but the DB port might be blocked by a firewall or the service isn’t running.

>These small checks can quickly narrow down whether it’s a network issue or an application/service issue.
